<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> Which religious order did Aquinas join, against the wishes of his family?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Dominicans</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Franciscans</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">Benedictines</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Jesuits</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Aquinas joined the Dominicans, a new mendicant order devoted to preaching and study. His family objected so strongly that they held him captive for about a year to change his mind.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Aquinas joined a mendicant order of preachers founded in the early thirteenth century. Revisit Aquinas' Life and Context for the story of his family's resistance.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> What is the name of the large work by Aquinas from which our readings are drawn?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">Confessions</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Summa Theologiae</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">Nicomachean Ethics</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">City of God</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Our readings come from the Summa Theologiae, Aquinas's massive and unfinished synthesis of theology and philosophy, organized as Questions divided into Articles.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Two of these titles belong to other authors we know: Aristotle and Augustine. Look for Aquinas's own systematic summary of theology.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> In an Article of the Summa, which part contains Aquinas's own position and argument?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">The question title</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">The "on the contrary" (sed contra)</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">The objections</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">The "I answer that" paragraph</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Each Article lists objections, then a 'sed contra' citing an authority, and then 'I answer that,' where Aquinas gives his own position and argument before replying to the objections.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The objections state views Aquinas will answer, and the sed contra cites an authority on the other side. Ask where in the Article Aquinas speaks in his own voice.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> Which of the following is NOT part of Aquinas's four-part definition of law from Question 90?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">It is made by someone with care of the community</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">It is directed toward the common good</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">It is enforced by penalties for non-compliance</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">It is an ordinance of reason</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Aquinas defines law as an ordinance of reason, for the common good, made by whoever has care of the community, and promulgated. Penalties may support law, but they are not part of what makes something law.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Aquinas's definition has four elements: reason, the common good, proper authority, and promulgation. Check which option names something outside that definition.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> According to Aquinas, what is the relationship between reason and revelation?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">They reveal the same truth through different means and cannot ultimately disagree</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">Revelation has been replaced by reason in the modern era</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">Reason is for pagans, revelation is for Christians</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">They are opposed, and when they conflict, faith must always prevail</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. For Aquinas, truth cannot contradict truth. Reason and revelation reach the same truth by different routes, so an apparent conflict means an error has crept in somewhere.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Aquinas was not a fideist who set faith against reason. Revisit Reason and Faith: he holds that both come from the same source and so cannot ultimately disagree.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> Which of the following is one of the four kinds of law Aquinas identifies in Question 91?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Canon law</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Eternal law</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Royal law</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">Customary law</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Aquinas identifies four kinds of law in Question 91: eternal, natural, human, and divine.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Question 91 names four kinds of law: eternal, natural, human, and divine. Only one of these options appears on that list.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> For Aquinas, what is the eternal law?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">The ancient customs of the Roman Empire</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">The moral code revealed in the Ten Commandments</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">The rational plan of the divine mind governing all creation</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">The laws of nature as studied by physicists</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Eternal law is the plan of divine wisdom by which God governs the whole of creation. Every other kind of law derives from it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Eternal law is more fundamental than any written code or scientific law of nature. Ask what Aquinas thinks ultimately orders all of creation.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> What is the relationship between natural law and eternal law, according to Aquinas?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Natural law is older than eternal law and sets the limits on what God can command</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">Natural law and eternal law are two names for exactly the same thing</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Natural law is a separate law that exists independently of eternal law</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">Natural law is the eternal law as it applies to rational creatures through their reason</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Natural law is rational creatures' participation in the eternal law. Through reason, human beings grasp part of the divine plan as it applies to them.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Natural law and eternal law are related but not identical. Aquinas's word is 'participation': ask how rational creatures share in the divine plan.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> According to Aquinas, human law derives its authority from:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">The consent of the governed</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">Natural law, from which it must be derived</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">Its power to enforce compliance</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">Tradition and long-standing custom</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A human law is legitimate insofar as it is derived from natural law. A law that contradicts natural law lacks the nature of law, which is the basis for Aquinas's claim that an unjust law is no law at all.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">For Aquinas, neither force nor custom nor consent is the deepest source of a law's authority. Ask what a just human law must be derived from.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> Which of the following is NOT one of the three layers of natural inclination Aquinas identifies in Question 94?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">The inclination we share with angels toward contemplating God directly</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">The inclination we have as rational creatures toward knowing truth and living in community</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">The inclination we share with other animals toward reproduction and raising young</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">The inclination we share with all substances toward self-preservation</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Aquinas's three levels are inclinations we share with all substances (self-preservation), with animals (reproduction and raising young), and those proper to rational beings (knowing truth and living in society). Contemplating God directly with the angels is not one of them.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Question 94 builds natural inclinations up in three layers, from what we share with all things, to what we share with animals, to what is proper to reason. Find the option that is not one of those layers.</div>
  </div>
</div>

